Path: pluto.uk03.bull.co.uk!know!cass.ma02.bull.com!think.com!spool.mu.edu!agate!usenet.ins.cwru.edu!gatech!rutgers!cbmvax!chrisg
From: chrisg@cbmvax.commodore.com (Chris Green)
Newsgroups: comp.sys.amiga.applications
Subject: Re: More 4000 compatible software
Message-ID: <36761@cbmvax.commodore.com>
Date: 6 Nov 92 14:17:23 GMT
References: <Bx8yKI.BFE@fc.hp.com> <David_Corn.0ii2@fcircus.sat.tx.us>
Reply-To: chrisg@cbmvax.commodore.com (Chris Green)
Organization: Commodore, West Chester, PA
Lines: 27

In article <David_Corn.0ii2@fcircus.sat.tx.us> David_Corn@fcircus.sat.tx.us (David Corn) writes:
>
>In <Bx8yKI.BFE@fc.hp.com>, koren@fc.hp.com (Steve Koren) writes:
>> When you scroll the starfield, *wham!*.  You get a new display right
>> away, instead of having to wait like on a non-AGA machine.
>> 
>>  - steve
>
>
>D*mn braggart..grin!  How about some WritePixel and other AIBB4.x
>statistics?  Can you give us any numbers?
>
	Note that the AIBB WritePixel test is no longer a good benchmark
_for HARDWARE_. The 3.0 ROM in the A4000 and A1200 contain a new optimized
WritePixel routine which can run around 3 times faster. Running this
test on a 25mhz A3000 with a 3.0 kickstart shows it being 3 times faster
than a 25mhz A3000 in AIBB! There are also other graphics speedups on the
AGA machines which come from software, not hardware.

